{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small, independently operated services can be MORE resilient to correlated failure than one large service with strictly more internal redundancy, provided the dominant failure mode is control-plane coordination rather than individual component failure. The strict total-redundancy advantage of the monolith is a red herring when failures are governed by central misconfiguration or coordination collapse.","counterconditions":["Failures are dominated by component-level wear-out rather than control-plane decisions, in which case the monolith's strict redundancy advantage dominates.","The decentralized services are operationally homogeneous (e.g., all using the same cloud provider or same deploy tooling), collapsing the independence of the control planes and recreating monolithic correlated risk.","The monolith architecturally isolates its redundant components across independent control planes internally (e.g., via strict cell-based architecture), negating the decentralization advantage."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Large monolithic services concentrate control over all redundant components in a single control plane. A single logical error (e.g., a bad deploy, a centralized misconfiguration, or a shared dependency update) can simultaneously disable all internally redundant paths, making the mathematical redundancy moot. In a decentralized network, redundant capacity is partitioned across independent control planes (different teams, deploy schedules, and operational practices). A fault originating in one control plane is bounded and cannot propagate across the entire system's redundancy. Thus, even with strictly less total component redundancy, the decentralized network survives correlated control-plane faults the monolith cannot.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["eb4515296f5292fab30875a85afa5f21db8fbdea9615468bb2d07d0cc19c62e2","09a3c8106b138005252dd72e7642b00f65aa891fecff6e3fa95b043a5b98435b"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}